  { q: 'Why are raven feathers iridescent?', a: 'Raven feathers appear iridescent — shifting between black, blue-violet, blue-green, and bronze — because of microscopic surface structures in the feather barbules that cause structural colouration through light interference, in addition to the base dark pigmentation from eumelanin. The iridescence is angle-dependent: the same feather appears different colours from different viewing angles. This structural colouration produces colours beyond what pigment alone could achieve and is a signal of feather quality and overall condition in corvids.' },
